WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:17.365
I was hoping today to talk
a little bit about creativity.

00:00:17.389 --> 00:00:19.359
You know, a lot of people really struggle

00:00:19.383 --> 00:00:21.602
to give themselves
permission to be creative.

00:00:21.626 --> 00:00:22.885
And reasonably so.

00:00:22.909 --> 00:00:25.666
I mean, we're all a little suspect
of our own talent.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:29.670
And I remember a story
I came across in my early 20s

00:00:29.694 --> 00:00:31.984
that kind of meant a lot to me.

00:00:32.008 --> 00:00:33.637
I was really into Allen Ginsberg,

00:00:33.661 --> 00:00:35.090
and I was reading his poetry,

00:00:35.114 --> 00:00:37.899
and I was reading --
he did a lot of interviews --

00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:42.234
and one time, William F. Buckley
had this television program

00:00:42.258 --> 00:00:43.798
called "Firing Line,"

00:00:43.822 --> 00:00:47.777
and Ginsberg went on there
and sang a Hare Krishna song

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.895
while playing the harmonium.

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:52.833
And he got back to New York
to all his intelligentsia friends,

00:00:52.857 --> 00:00:54.008
and they all told him,

00:00:54.032 --> 00:00:56.591
"Don't you know that everybody
thinks you're an idiot,

00:00:56.615 --> 00:00:58.726
and the whole country's
making fun of you?"

00:00:59.369 --> 00:01:01.590
And he said, "That's my job.

00:01:02.679 --> 00:01:05.310
I'm a poet, and I'm going
to play the fool.

00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:07.764
Most people have to go
to work all day long,

00:01:07.788 --> 00:01:10.242
and they come home
and they fight with their spouse,

00:01:10.266 --> 00:01:12.613
and they eat, and they turn on
the old boob tube,

00:01:12.637 --> 00:01:14.608
and somebody tries
to sell them something,

00:01:14.632 --> 00:01:16.154
and I just screwed all that up.

00:01:16.178 --> 00:01:18.353
I went on and I sang about Krishna,

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:22.102
and now they're sitting in bed
and going, 'Who is this stupid poet?'

00:01:22.126 --> 00:01:23.839
And they can't fall asleep, right?"

00:01:23.863 --> 00:01:26.091
And that's his job as a poet.

00:01:26.115 --> 00:01:27.947
And so, I find that very liberating,

00:01:27.971 --> 00:01:31.936
because I think that most of us
really want to offer the world

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.735
something of quality,

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:37.219
something that the world
will consider good or important.

00:01:37.243 --> 00:01:40.223
And that's really the enemy,

00:01:40.247 --> 00:01:44.845
because it's not up to us
whether what we do is any good,

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:46.931
and if history has taught us anything,

00:01:46.955 --> 00:01:49.702
the world is an extremely
unreliable critic.

00:01:50.178 --> 00:01:51.333
Right?

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.177
So you have to ask yourself:

00:01:53.891 --> 00:01:57.544
Do you think human creativity matters?

00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:00.644
Well, hmm.

00:02:00.668 --> 00:02:04.966
Most people don't spend a lot of time
thinking about poetry. Right?

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:06.215
They have a life to live,

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:09.349
and they're not really that concerned
with Allen Ginsberg's poems

00:02:09.373 --> 00:02:10.699
or anybody's poems,

00:02:10.723 --> 00:02:13.270
until their father dies,

00:02:13.294 --> 00:02:14.479
they go to a funeral,

00:02:14.503 --> 00:02:16.461
you lose a child,

00:02:16.485 --> 00:02:19.139
somebody breaks your heart,
they don't love you anymore,

00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:20.334
and all of a sudden,

00:02:20.358 --> 00:02:24.186
you're desperate for making sense
out of this life,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.629
and, "Has anybody ever
felt this bad before?

00:02:27.653 --> 00:02:29.457
How did they come out of this cloud?"

00:02:29.481 --> 00:02:31.258
Or the inverse -- something great.

00:02:31.282 --> 00:02:33.321
You meet somebody and your heart explodes.

00:02:33.345 --> 00:02:36.166
You love them so much,
you can't even see straight.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:37.363
You know, you're dizzy.

00:02:37.387 --> 00:02:40.364
"Did anybody feel like this before?
What is happening to me?"

00:02:40.388 --> 00:02:44.937
And that's when art's not a luxury,
it's actually sustenance.

00:02:44.961 --> 00:02:46.151
We need it.

00:02:46.175 --> 00:02:48.299
OK. Well, what is it?

00:02:48.323 --> 00:02:53.548
Human creativity
is nature manifest in us.

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:56.416
We look at the, oh ...

00:02:57.563 --> 00:02:58.990
the aurora borealis. Right?

00:02:59.014 --> 00:03:01.579
I did this movie called
"White Fang" when I was a kid,

00:03:01.603 --> 00:03:02.813
and we shot up in Alaska,

00:03:02.837 --> 00:03:03.991
and you go out at night

00:03:04.015 --> 00:03:07.455
and the sky was like rippling
with purple and pink and white,

00:03:07.479 --> 00:03:09.607
and it's the most beautiful
thing I ever saw.

00:03:09.631 --> 00:03:11.871
It really looked like the sky was playing.

00:03:11.895 --> 00:03:13.046
Beautiful.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:14.699
You go to Grand Canyon at sundown.

00:03:14.723 --> 00:03:15.874
It's beautiful.

00:03:15.898 --> 00:03:17.123
We know that's beautiful.

00:03:17.147 --> 00:03:18.652
But fall in love?

00:03:18.676 --> 00:03:20.140
Your lover's pretty beautiful.

00:03:20.164 --> 00:03:21.851
I have four kids.

00:03:21.875 --> 00:03:23.399
Watching them play?

00:03:23.423 --> 00:03:25.691
Watching them pretend to be a butterfly

00:03:25.715 --> 00:03:27.888
or run around the house
and doing anything,

00:03:27.912 --> 00:03:30.248
it's so beautiful.

00:03:30.272 --> 00:03:34.411
And I believe that we are here
on this star in space

00:03:34.435 --> 00:03:37.015
to try to help one another. Right?

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:39.461
And first we have to survive,

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:40.848
and then we have to thrive.

00:03:40.872 --> 00:03:44.206
And to thrive, to express ourselves,

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.113
alright, well, here's the rub:
we have to know ourselves.

00:03:47.137 --> 00:03:48.500
What do you love?

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.156
And if you get close to what you love,

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.892
who you are is revealed to you,

00:03:52.916 --> 00:03:54.669
and it expands.

00:03:54.693 --> 00:03:56.416
For me, it was really easy.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.024
I did my first professional play.
I was 12 years old.

00:03:59.048 --> 00:04:01.881
I was in a play called "Saint Joan"
by George Bernard Shaw

00:04:01.905 --> 00:04:03.261
at the McCarter Theatre,

00:04:03.285 --> 00:04:05.193
and -- boom! -- I was in love.

00:04:05.908 --> 00:04:07.923
My world just expanded.

00:04:08.345 --> 00:04:10.491
And that profession --
I'm almost 50 now --

00:04:10.515 --> 00:04:13.293
that profession has never stopped
giving back to me,

00:04:13.317 --> 00:04:15.349
and it gives back more and more,

00:04:15.373 --> 00:04:17.966
mostly, strangely,

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:19.943
through the characters that I've played.

00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:22.997
I've played cops, I've played criminals,

00:04:23.021 --> 00:04:25.645
I've played priests, I've played sinners,

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:30.396
and the magic of this over a lifetime,
over 30 years of doing this,

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.285
is that you start to see
that my experiences,

00:04:33.309 --> 00:04:36.685
me, Ethan, is not nearly as unique

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:37.860
as I thought.

00:04:37.884 --> 00:04:40.923
I have so much in common
with all these people.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:43.763
And so they have something
in common with me.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.506
You start to see
how connected we all are.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.255
My great-grandmother,
Della Hall Walker Green,

00:04:52.279 --> 00:04:54.105
on her deathbed,

00:04:54.129 --> 00:04:57.844
she wrote this little biography
in the hospital,

00:04:57.868 --> 00:05:00.655
and it was only about 36 pages long,

00:05:00.679 --> 00:05:04.034
and she spent about five pages

00:05:04.058 --> 00:05:07.167
on the one time
she did costumes for a play.

00:05:07.191 --> 00:05:10.337
Her first husband got, like, a paragraph.

00:05:10.361 --> 00:05:15.355
Cotton farming, of which
she did for 50 years, gets a mention.

00:05:15.379 --> 00:05:18.366
Five pages on doing these costumes.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.829
And I look -- my mom gave me
one of her quilts that she made,

00:05:21.853 --> 00:05:23.034
and you can feel it.

00:05:23.058 --> 00:05:24.937
She was expressing herself,

00:05:24.961 --> 00:05:26.961
and it has a power that's real.

00:05:26.985 --> 00:05:30.721
I remember my stepbrother and I
went to go see "Top Gun,"

00:05:30.745 --> 00:05:32.336
whatever year that came out.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.587
And I remember we walked out of the mall,
it was, like, blazing hot,

00:05:35.611 --> 00:05:36.806
I just looked at him,

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.622
and we both felt that movie
just like a calling from God.

00:05:40.646 --> 00:05:42.437
You know? Just ...

00:05:42.521 --> 00:05:43.826
But completely differently.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.299
Like, I wanted to be an actor.

00:05:45.299 --> 00:05:48.210
I was like, I've got to make something
that makes people feel.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.881
I just want to be a part of that.

00:05:49.881 --> 00:05:51.605
And he wanted to be in the military.

00:05:51.605 --> 00:05:54.027
That's all we ever did
was play FBI, play army man,

00:05:54.027 --> 00:05:56.718
play knights, you know,
and I'd like, pose with my sword,

00:05:56.718 --> 00:05:58.496
and he would build a working crossbow

00:05:58.496 --> 00:06:00.509
that you could shoot an arrow into a tree.

00:06:00.629 --> 00:06:01.780
So he joins the army.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.430
Well, he just retired
a colonel in the Green Berets.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:08.713
He's a multidecorated combat veteran
of Afghanistan and Iraq.

00:06:08.737 --> 00:06:12.239
He now teaches a sail camp
for children of fallen soldiers.

00:06:12.263 --> 00:06:13.940
He gave his life to his passion.

00:06:13.964 --> 00:06:16.505
His creativity was leadership,

00:06:16.529 --> 00:06:17.708
leading others,

00:06:17.732 --> 00:06:19.075
his bravery, to help others.

00:06:19.099 --> 00:06:21.483
That was something he felt called to do,

00:06:21.507 --> 00:06:23.634
and it gave back to him.

00:06:23.658 --> 00:06:26.215
We know this -- the time
of our life is so short,

00:06:26.239 --> 00:06:27.420
and how we spend it --

00:06:27.444 --> 00:06:30.817
are we spending it
doing what's important to us?

00:06:30.841 --> 00:06:32.422
Most of us not.

00:06:32.446 --> 00:06:34.028
I mean, it's hard.

00:06:34.052 --> 00:06:36.512
The pull of habit is so huge,

00:06:36.536 --> 00:06:39.115
and that's what makes kids
so beautifully creative,

00:06:39.139 --> 00:06:41.087
is that they don't have any habits,

00:06:41.111 --> 00:06:43.676
and they don't care
if they're any good or not, right?

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.687
They're not building a sandcastle going,

00:06:45.711 --> 00:06:49.286
"I think I'm going to be
a really good sandcastle builder."

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:53.218
They just throw themselves at whatever
project you put in front of them --

00:06:53.242 --> 00:06:55.015
dancing, doing a painting,

00:06:55.039 --> 00:06:56.450
building something:

00:06:56.474 --> 00:06:57.988
any opportunity they have,

00:06:58.012 --> 00:07:02.615
they try to use it to impress upon you
their individuality.

00:07:03.107 --> 00:07:04.465
It's so beautiful.

00:07:05.107 --> 00:07:08.642
It's a thing that worries me sometimes
whenever you talk about creativity,

00:07:08.666 --> 00:07:11.710
because it can have this kind of feel
that it's just nice,

00:07:11.734 --> 00:07:15.158
you know, or it's warm
or it's something pleasant.

00:07:15.182 --> 00:07:16.341
It's not.

00:07:16.365 --> 00:07:17.682
It's vital.

00:07:17.706 --> 00:07:20.514
It's the way we heal each other.

00:07:20.538 --> 00:07:22.632
In singing our song,

00:07:22.656 --> 00:07:24.271
in telling our story,

00:07:24.295 --> 00:07:25.677
in inviting you to say,

00:07:25.701 --> 00:07:28.404
"Hey, listen to me,
and I'll listen to you,"

00:07:28.428 --> 00:07:30.796
we're starting a dialogue.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.629
And when you do that,
this healing happens,

00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:35.463
and we come out of our corners,

00:07:35.487 --> 00:07:38.434
and we start to witness
each other's common humanity.

00:07:38.458 --> 00:07:39.902
We start to assert it.

00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:43.258
And when we do that,
really good things happen.

00:07:43.282 --> 00:07:46.705
So, if you want to help your community,
if you want to help your family,

00:07:46.729 --> 00:07:48.340
if you want to help your friends,

00:07:48.364 --> 00:07:50.385
you have to express yourself.

00:07:50.409 --> 00:07:52.992
And to express yourself,
you have to know yourself.

00:07:53.619 --> 00:07:55.333
It's actually super easy.

00:07:55.796 --> 00:07:58.193
You just have to follow your love.

00:07:58.217 --> 00:08:00.352
There is no path.

00:08:00.975 --> 00:08:03.569
There's no path till you walk it,

00:08:03.593 --> 00:08:06.226
and you have to be willing
to play the fool.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:09.552
So don't read the book
that you should read,

00:08:09.576 --> 00:08:11.389
read the book you want to read.

00:08:11.413 --> 00:08:14.796
Don't listen to the music
that you used to like.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.110
Take some time to listen
to some new music.

00:08:17.134 --> 00:08:20.651
Take some time to talk to somebody
that you don't normally talk to.

00:08:20.675 --> 00:08:22.606
I guarantee, if you do that,

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:24.298
you will feel foolish.

00:08:24.973 --> 00:08:26.475
That's the point.

00:08:26.499 --> 00:08:27.960
Play the fool.

00:08:49.701 --> 00:08:52.760
(Plays guitar)

00:08:54.585 --> 00:08:58.499
(Sings) Well, I want to go Austin,
and I wanna stay home.

00:08:58.523 --> 00:09:01.737
Invite our friends over
but still be alone.

00:09:01.761 --> 00:09:03.408
Live for danger.

00:09:03.432 --> 00:09:04.950
Play it cool.

00:09:04.974 --> 00:09:09.114
Have everyone respect me
for being a fool.

